          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In a 6-month-old infant, which clinical presentation is most characteristic of u</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In a 6-month-old infant, which clinical presentation is most characteristic of urinary tract infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dysuria and urinary frequency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dysuria and frequency are lower-UTI (cystitis) symptoms seen in children older than 2 years who can localise and report urinary discomfort. Infants cannot demonstrate these symptoms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fever without an identifiable source</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In infants aged 3 months to 2 years, fever without an identifiable source is the cardinal presentation of UTI. Classic urinary symptoms cannot be elicited at this age, making UTI a critical differential in any febrile infant.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Flank pain with costovertebral angle tenderness</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Flank pain and costovertebral angle tenderness are signs of upper UTI (pyelonephritis) in older children and adolescents who can localise pain — not in a 6-month-old.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Suprapubic discomfort with increased urinary frequency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Suprapubic discomfort and frequency are cystitis symptoms reported by older, verbal children. Infants present with non-specific features such as fever, poor feeding, and irritability.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key point is that infants aged 3 months to 2 years cannot report urinary symptoms. Fever without an identifiable source is the cardinal presentation — UTI must be considered in every febrile infant."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Fever without a source is the single most important clue to UTI in pre-verbal infants — always include urine dipstick in the work-up of unexplained fever at this age."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
