          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 4-year-old child receiving aminoglycoside therapy for sepsis has rising serum </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 4-year-old child receiving aminoglycoside therapy for sepsis has rising serum creatinine over 48 hours, but urine output remains at 1.5 mL/kg/h. Which interpretation is most appropriate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The child does not have AKI because urine output is preserved</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Preserved urine output does not exclude AKI. Non-oliguric AKI is a well-recognised entity — relying solely on urine output will miss these cases and delay management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The rising creatinine suggests chronic rather than acute kidney disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A rising creatinine over 48 hours in the context of aminoglycoside exposure indicates acute injury. CKD develops insidiously over months to years with gradual deterioration, not an acute rise.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The creatinine rise is likely a laboratory artefact and should be repeated before acting</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dismissing a rising creatinine as laboratory error in a child on a known nephrotoxin is clinically dangerous. Aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity should be evaluated immediately.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The child has non-oliguric AKI, which is particularly associated with aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Non-oliguric AKI — where kidney function deteriorates despite preserved urine output — occurs especially with aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity. Rising creatinine is the key indicator; oliguria is not required for diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key concept is non-oliguric AKI: kidney function can decline significantly even when urine output appears adequate. This pattern is particularly characteristic of aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity — always monitor creatinine in children on nephrotoxic drugs."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Non-oliguric AKI is an important clinical pitfall — aminoglycosides damage tubular concentrating ability while initially preserving urine volume, so creatinine trends are essential for early detection."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
