          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the most common presenting feature of Wilms tumour in children?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the most common presenting feature of Wilms tumour in children?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Asymptomatic abdominal mass discovered incidentally</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An incidentally discovered painless abdominal mass — usually noticed by a parent during bathing or dressing, or by a clinician on routine examination — is the hallmark presentation. The child typically appears completely well despite a large tumour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Painless gross haematuria</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While haematuria can occur in Wilms tumour, it is not the most common presenting feature. An incidentally discovered abdominal mass in a well child is far more typical.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Persistent abdominal pain with constitutional symptoms</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Persistent pain and constitutional symptoms suggest advanced disease or a different diagnosis. Wilms tumour characteristically presents as a painless mass in an otherwise healthy child.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Recurrent urinary tract infections</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recurrent UTIs are associated with structural urinary tract anomalies such as vesicoureteric reflux or posterior urethral valves, not with Wilms tumour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The cardinal presentation of Wilms tumour is an asymptomatic abdominal mass in a well-appearing child, discovered incidentally by a parent or clinician. The child's apparent good health despite a large tumour is a defining and deceptive characteristic."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The deceptive normality of the child — well, playful, growing — despite a large intra-abdominal mass is one of the most important clinical lessons about Wilms tumour."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
